INT. LOST UMBRELLA ARCHIVE - NIGHT

A cavernous basement beneath a railway station. Wooden racks stretch into darkness, each hung with hundreds of umbrellas, tagged and dated. A single desk lamp burns green. The air smells of damp wool and old rain.

INSPECTOR FINBAR HOLT, 50s, tweed, wire-rimmed spectacles, stands at the desk holding a long black umbrella by its handle. He turns it slowly in the lamplight.

CEDRIC LOWE, 60s, the archivist, thin, nervous, with cotton gloves, hovers by the register. He keeps wiping his palms against his trousers.

HOLT
Mr. Lowe. When was this deposited?

LOWE
I cannot say, Inspector. Our records--

HOLT
Please try.

LOWE
Tuesday, I think. Possibly Wednesday.

HOLT
It is stained.

LOWE
Many of them are stained.

HOLT
Not with this.

He tilts the umbrella. Along the ferrule, a dark crust. Lowe's gaze flicks to it and away.

HOLT (CONT'D)
You found it on the tracks.

LOWE
Platform four. After the last train.

HOLT
And you did not think to call us.

LOWE
It was an umbrella, Inspector. We receive eleven hundred a month.

HOLT
Do you read the papers?

LOWE
I... prefer the crossword.

HOLT
A man was found in the signal box on Tuesday morning. Head injury. No weapon.

LOWE
How dreadful.

HOLT
You seem calm.

LOWE
I am a calm man.

HOLT
Mr. Lowe, I think you are afraid.

Lowe says nothing. Holt lays the umbrella on the desk like a body on a slab.

A door CREAKS at the far end of the room. Footsteps echo among the racks.

MAGDA PRZYBYLSKI, 40s, wrapped in a wine-red coat, steps out of the dark. A leather satchel on her shoulder. Her hair is wet.

MAGDA
I believe that umbrella is mine.

HOLT
Is it, madam?

MAGDA
I left it on the platform. On Tuesday evening.

LOWE
Magda.

HOLT
You know each other?

LOWE
(quickly)
No.

MAGDA
(simultaneously)
Yes.

Holt takes off his spectacles. Cleans them on his cuff.

HOLT
Then perhaps one of you will tell me whose name is on the ticket stub in the handle.

Lowe's mouth opens. Magda glances at him. A look passes between them that Holt does not miss.

LOWE ^
It's not mine.

MAGDA
It's not mine.

HOLT
Then whose?

The desk lamp FLICKERS. In the dark, between the racks, an umbrella falls with a clatter.
